# Manuscript PDF page 53

                                 T H E H O N DA



   “You knew?”
   “I’ve watched you check the window before you open a laptop for
two years, Juan Carlos.” She said it gently. “I thought it was a supersti‐
tion. I didn’t say anything because I figured you’d tell me when you
wanted to.”
   I didn’t trust my voice.
   “Remember the mural?” she said. “In my bedroom? You painted the
whole wall in one night, no sleep, you were in a trance, it was gorgeous.
And the baseboard by the door never got done. It’s been three years.
There’s still tape on it.” A little laugh, the kind that’s mostly breath.
“And I used to think the tape was about me. That you’d lost interest. I
took it personally. We fought about it.”
   “Yari—”
   “No, let me finish. Because I figured it out, a while ago, that it wasn’t
about caring. I watched you care so hard about things you couldn’t fin‐
ish that it made you sick. I stopped taking it personally. I just didn’t
have the word for what I’d stopped taking personally.” Her voice
caught for half a second and went on. “Now you’re telling me at four in
the morning there’s a word. I could’ve told you. I think I’ve known
longer than you have. I just didn’t think it was my place to hand you a
diagnosis you hadn’t asked for.”
   Something was sitting at the back of my throat. I decided it was al‐
lergies.
   “Do the list with me,” I said.
   “I only got nine minutes.”
   “So do it fast.”
   She had one pulled up on her end, a clinical one, because she’s a
nurse and she doesn’t trust the Honda either. She read me four items.
Often fails to finish tasks. Often loses things necessary for tasks. Often fidgets
or taps hands or feet. Often avoids tasks requiring sustained effort until the
last minute. After each one, she waited. I either laughed or went quiet.
She’s learned in two years to tell the silences apart, which is no and
which is oh, God, that one. Every time I went quiet, she said, soft, “Yeah.
That one too, huh,” and wrote nothing down. She just held it with me.




